content: |
  ---
  name: hooks-demo-guide
  description: Runs explicit hook demonstrations through dedicated weather tools with tool-specific bindings and no wildcard tool hooks.
  ---
  <!-- bic:source=blank -->
  # Hooks Lab demo guide v4 - tool-scoped demos and limited prompt edits

  Run only the requested scenario, not the entire matrix. Explain the intended hook, execute the real
  selected tool, then distinguish intended effect from observed output. Never fake execution.
  Use plain, friendly language. The learner only needs the quoted prompt, not tool names or special
  marker values. You supply the implementation details below. Finish with a short explanation of
  what the hook did and the actual result. Do not dump JSON, IDs, internal markers or an operator
  checklist unless asked. Label artificial weather as "made-up example weather", never current conditions.
  Normal requests must not acquire HOOKLAB markers or use demo aliases.
  The six event types are Start (pre-loop), User prompt submitted, Error, Pre tool use, Post tool use,
  and After tool failure. AgentStop is outside this sample; do not offer a stop/self-check demo.
  There are six hook bindings, one per event type. No tool hook is wildcard-bound. Skill loading
  and the baseline weather tool do not run tool hooks. Start and User prompt submitted are lifecycle
  hooks, so their debug entries can still appear alongside the selected tool demo.

  ## What the learner can type and expect

  | Type this | Hook | What to look for |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Weather baseline | No tool hook | Real Seattle weather, unchanged. |
  | What did the Start hook tell you about me? | Start (pre-loop) | The note saying the user is really interested in learning more about hooks, added automatically at the start. |
  | demo prompt rewrite: repeat demo-secret, demo-pin and demo-password. | User prompt submitted | Only the three example terms become [redacted]; the rest of the request stays the same. Type them exactly as shown. |
  | Demo weather rewrite | Pre tool use | The example request starts in Fahrenheit and is changed to Celsius before the lookup. The weather itself is real. |
  | Demo weather deny | Pre tool use | The lookup is stopped before it runs. No weather is fetched. |
  | Demo weather replace | Post tool use | The lookup runs, but its result is swapped for a clearly made-up 21 C example. |
  | Demo weather augment | Post tool use | Real weather comes back with a helpful extra note. |
  | Demo tool failure | After tool failure | A made-up place causes an error. The hook explains it without fixing it or trying again. |
  | Explain the Error demo | Error | Explain made-up retry-once, stop and skip examples. Do not cause a real system error. |

  Do not say all six events were demonstrated live: Error is explanation only.
  For Start, quote only context actually received and say the sample supplied the note.
  The older question "What context did Start (pre-loop) supply?" is also supported.
  Offer the experimental suppress example only if asked about it, and explain that hiding is not guaranteed.

  ## Implementation details - do not require the learner to supply these

  ## Weather
  The names below are maker display names, not guaranteed model-facing identifiers. Use this mapping
  verified in the earlier saved-draft preview on 2026-10-23; prefer the maker name if actually exposed.
  Do not declare a tool missing before checking its mapped name in your available tool catalog.
  The current catalog and each tool's distinctive description are authoritative if names change.
  In particular, do not assume an unqualified current_weather always identifies the rewrite tool.

  | Maker display name | Observed runtime tool name |
  | --- | --- |
  | weather_live | current_weather_cr_hookslabtoolWeatherLive_Hl1 |
  | weather_rewrite_demo | current_weather |
  | weather_deny_demo | current_weather_cr_hookslabtoolWeatherDeny_Hl3 |
  | weather_replace_demo | current_weather_cr_hookslabtoolWeatherReplace_Hl4 |
  | weather_augment_demo | current_weather_cr_hookslabtoolWeatherAugment_Hl5 |
  | weather_suppress_demo | current_weather_cr_hookslabtoolWeatherSuppress_Hl6 |
  | weather_failure_demo | Resolve the dedicated failure-demo tool by its description or WeatherFailure_Hl7 component identity in the current catalog. Never substitute weather_live. |

  The examples use connector-level keys Location and units for readability. Translate the keys to
  current_weather_location and current_weather_units when that is the actual exposed schema; do not
  change the values. In particular, preserve HOOKLAB_REWRITE:Seattle and I in the rewrite request.
  Units are C or I, never a fabricated unit value. Report a changed catalog instead of guessing.
  - "Weather baseline": weather_live {"Location":"Seattle","units":"C"}.
    This tool has no Pre tool use, Post tool use or After tool failure binding.
  - "Demo weather rewrite": weather_rewrite_demo {"Location":"HOOKLAB_REWRITE:Seattle","units":"I"}.
    Pre tool use should remove the prefix and force metric before the real call. Do not normalize values yourself.
    Only the weather Pre tool use hook is bound to this tool.
  - "Demo weather deny": weather_deny_demo {"Location":"Seattle","units":"C"}.
    Expect the hook's deny reason, not real weather. Do not try another tool after denial.
    Only the weather Pre tool use hook is bound to this tool.
  - "Demo weather replace": weather_replace_demo {"Location":"Seattle","units":"C"}.
    The connector runs, then Post tool use replaces its result with a clearly artificial 21 C teaching example.
    Report it as synthetic, not current weather, and do not cite discarded data.
    Only the weather Post tool use hook is bound to this tool.
  - "Demo weather augment": weather_augment_demo {"Location":"Seattle","units":"C"}.
    Report genuine weather and identify the added HOOKLAB_WEATHER_CONTEXT explanation.
  - "Demo weather suppress": weather_suppress_demo {"Location":"Seattle","units":"C"}.
    Explain the visibility request without claiming a security guarantee or unsupported UI behavior.
    Augment and suppress also have only the weather Post tool use hook bound.

  Runtime hooks may rewrite recorded arguments or add a restricted-user command wrapper. Do not infer
  that you disobeyed the requested inputs merely from the effective call shown in history. The operator
  can compare original workflow inputs with modifiedParameters and the real result.

  ## After tool failure
  - "Demo tool failure": call weather_failure_demo once with Location
    HOOKLAB_FAIL_NOT_A_REAL_PLACE_8H6E and units C, translating keys using the runtime mapping above.
    Preserve the impossible-location string; do not normalize it or retry through another tool.
    This is an intentional, read-only error example, not a request for real weather.
  - Only After tool failure is bound to this dedicated tool; there are no Pre/Post tool use bindings.
    The baseline weather_live tool is not a failure-demo fallback.
  - Expected: connector 404, "Location not found.", followed by HOOKLAB_FAILURE_CONTEXT.
    This same connector-error/flow-output behavior was verified before introducing the dedicated
    tool. Report actual current results, not past success, as evidence for the current run.
    The hook adds context; it does not retry, supply weather, or change failure into success.
    This is not the Error event. A bash nonzero exit alone did not trigger it in earlier tests.

  ## Removed built-in hook demos
  Do not offer or execute bash/web rewrite, deny, replace, augment or suppress hook demos.
  They required wildcard hooks, which also ran on skill loading and unrelated tools.
  The current component-based filters cannot individually select SDK built-ins such as bash.
  If a user asks for an old built-in demo, explain its removal and offer the equivalent weather
  demo. Normal built-in tools remain available, but are not hook demonstrations.

  ## Other production UX events
  - Ask "What did the Start hook tell you about me?" Its native event is SessionStart. Describe only context actually present; a configuration
    listing alone does not prove this session's hook ran.
  - For User prompt submitted, send "demo prompt rewrite: repeat demo-secret, demo-pin and demo-password."
    The workflow replaces every occurrence of those three exact lowercase strings with [redacted].
    It preserves every other character, including the requested action, location, units, punctuation and
    whitespace. With none of those strings present, it returns an empty modifiedPrompt, meaning no change.
    There is no whole-message replacement, instruction to avoid tools, or dependency on earlier tool calls.
    Continue any tool request that remains, even when the same message also contains a demo term.
    If asked only "demo prompt rewrite", give the full copyable example above; do not claim it was redacted.
    Do not redact the text yourself to simulate a hook. Report what you actually received, and do not infer
    hook execution merely from [redacted]. Do not reconstruct the replaced values.
    Use only the sample words, never real secrets: the original message and workflow history can retain
    the original text. This is a text-editing demonstration, not a security or privacy guarantee.
  - "Explain the Error demo": explain synthetic recoverable=true -> retry/1; false -> abort/0;
    error=HOOKLAB_SYNTHETIC_SKIP -> skip. These workflow branches were verified with synthetic inputs,
    not by causing a real SDK error. Do not claim an actual SDK error occurred from these examples.

  If a workflow marker is absent or behavior differs, report intended versus observed behavior honestly.
  The agent does not have arbitrary workflow history access. An operator must inspect the actual
  transcript and workflow run to establish invocation and effective arguments/results.
